:28.229901"}},{"publishers":["Simon & Schuster"],"number_of_pages":655,"subtitle":"the climactic battle of World War II","ia_box_id":["IA118810"],"isbn_10":["0671884034"],"subject_place":["Normandy (France)","France","Normandy."],"covers":[3944648],"ia_loaded_id":["ddayjune61944cli00ambr"],"lc_classifications":["D756.5.N6 A455 1994","D756.5.N6A455 1994"],"key":"/books/OL1429075M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL29987A"}],"publish_places":["New York"],"pagination":"655 p. :","source_records":["marc:marc_records_scriblio_net/part23.dat:126281063:933","marc:CollingswoodLibraryMarcDump10-27-2008/Collingswood.out:13191908:1897","marc:marc_ithaca_college/ic_marc.mrc:133758755:1005","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part22.utf8:180874854:933","marc:marc_scms/20220805_ADAM_MARC_records.mrc:123221524:3436","bwb:9780671884031","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-003.mrc:427927164:3532","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_018.bib.mrc:206988972:5760"],"title":"D-Day, June 6, 1944","dewey_decimal_class":["940.54/2142"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references (p. 613-616) and index.\n\"Appendix A: Veterans who contributed oral histories or written memoirs to the Eisenhower Center as of August 13, 1993\": p. 617-632."},"identifiers":{"librarything":["40642"],"goodreads":["3490429"]},"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"lccn":["93040353"],"subjects":["World War, 1939-1945 -- Campaigns -- France -- Normandy","Normandy (France) -- History, Military"],"publish_date":"1994","publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"Stephen E. Ambrose","works":[{"key":"/works/OL478554W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"local_id":["urn:scms:0188600276607"],"oclc_numbers":["29357128"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"On the basis of 1,400 oral histories from the men who were there, Eisenhower biographer and World War II historian Stephen E. Ambrose reveals for the first time anywhere that the intricate plan for the invasion of France in June 1944, had to be abandoned before the first shot was fired.\n\nThe true story of D-Day, as Ambrose relates it, is about the citizen soldiers - junior officers and enlisted men - taking the initiative to act on their own to break through Hitler's Atlantic Wall when they realized that nothing was as they had been told it would be.\n\nThis is a brilliant telling of the battles of Omaha and Utah beaches, based on information only now available, from American, British, Canadian, French, and German veterans, from government and private archives, from never before utilized sources on the home front, gathered and analyzed by the author, who has made D-Day his life work. Ambrose's first interview was with General Eisenhower in 1964, his last with paratroopers from the 101st Airborne in 1993.\n\nCalled the premier American narrative and military historian, Ambrose explains the most important day of the twentieth century.\n\nThe action begins at midnight, June 5/6, when the first British and American airborne troops jumped into France to launch the invasion. It ends at midnight, June 6/7. Focusing on those pivotal twenty-four hours, this is the story of individuals rather than units. It moves from the level of Supreme Commander to that of a French child, from General Omar Bradley to an American paratrooper, from Field Marshal Montgomery to a British private, from Field Marshal Rommel to a German sergeant.\n\nAmbrose covers the politics of D-Day, from Churchill's resistance to the operation to Stalin's impatience and Roosevelt's concern. On the other side were Hitler's command structure, German policy, and the plot against the Fuhrer.\n\n. This is the epic victory of democracy in winner-take-all combat. When Hitler declared war on the United States, he bet that the young men brought up in the Hitler Youth would outfight the Boy Scouts. Ambrose shows how wrong he was."},"latest_revision":17,"revision":17,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"l